tonight we continue our regulation  nation series with a look at how  president Trump's massive infrastructure  plan could result in significant cutting  of federal red tape correspondent Doug  mackelway tells us how that would work  the Bayonne Bridge is a symbol of what  ails us infrastructure in 2009 the first  environmental impact studies were  undertaken to raise this roadway to  allow tall ships to pass underneath but  the studies alone took five years and  generated 10,000 pages of environmental  assessments and another 10,000 pages of  permitting the project is not slated for  completion until 2019 we built the  Empire State Building in just one year  the Trump administration is trying to  cut through such red tape as it proposes  a 1.5 trillion dollar infrastructure  program the political will has not been  there because you've got to take on a  lot of lawyers a lot of environmental  groups but this president has shown that  he's not afraid of those fights four  days after his inauguration the  president signed a memorandum to begin  streamlining the White House side of  this study as its roadmap it found the  permitting process so cumbersome it  sometimes harms the environment by  delaying construction of quote America's  antiquated power grid for example waste  the equivalent of 200 coal burning power  plants or 150 billion dollars per year  in subsequent memoranda the White House  promised to establish a one agency one  decision structure for environmental  review shorten the lengthy environmental  review process to two years and  eliminate redundant provisions in  environmental law  environmentalists and their Washington  ally say it's a wrong approach I would  never argue for a slower permitting  process  but the way to make it faster is to fund  the agencies that already have the  charge of putting permits through the  system the Trump plan also gives more  power to the states who've learned they  need to make permitting easier to be  competitive the states in many ways have  loved the way they often move toward  things like one-stop shopping so-called  concierge services  even so Trump's infrastructure promises  will be hard to keep after he gave the  go-ahead to complete the Keystone XL  pipeline a year ago it remains  unfinished and environmentalists filed a  new lawsuit this week to block it right  Doug thank you
 

